---
id: host-remote-01
courseId: frontend-architecture
title: "Host and ==remote=="
concepts: [host-and-remote, runtime-remote-loading, remote-entry-manifest, request-order]
# Sources for every claim about tools (checked 2026-10-06; quoted text was read on the linked page that day).
# Claim register: content/frontend-architecture/sources.md. Runtime output: examples/recorded/outputs.json and the
# recordings listed below.
# - Module Federation glossary: "An application that exposes other modules to be consumed by other JavaScript applications
#   through the Module Federation build plugin with the exposes configuration is referred to as a Provider (Producer)";
#   the heading is "Producer (Remote)". An application that consumes modules "through the Module Federation build plugin
#   with the remotes configuration" is the "Consumer (Host)":
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/start/glossary.html
# - exposes: "A producer-specific parameter, setting exposes indicates that this is a producer." "The exposed modules will
#   be separated into a distinct chunk":
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/exposes.html
# - remotes: "ModuleFederationInfo is composed of ModuleFederation name + @ + ModuleFederation entry"; "ModuleFederation
#   name is the name set by the producer"; "entry can be either mf-manifest.json or remoteEntry.js"; with
#   mf-manifest.json the extra capabilities are dynamic module type hints, resource preloading and the Chrome devtool:
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/remotes.html
# - Manifest: "mf-manifest.json is a runtime manifest generated by a producer after build. It describes which modules the
#   producer exposes, where the remote entry is, which assets those modules need, which shared dependencies are
#   available". The flow: "Host configures remotes -> request mf-manifest.json -> parse Manifest -> generate Snapshot ->
#   load remoteEntry, expose, shared, and preload assets":
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html
# - The plugin emits "mf-manifest.json and mf-stats.json ... on every build":
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/manifest.html
# - Loading by alias plus expose name ("alias + expose", for example loadRemote('app1/util')); the remotes page says an
#   alias "can be configured as needed" and used as import xx from 'demo':
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/runtime/runtime-api.html
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/remotes.html
# - Rsbuild plugin, remotes written as 'remote1@http://localhost:2001/mf-manifest.json':
#   https://module-federation.io/integrations/build-tool/rsbuild.html
# - Error code RUNTIME-003, "Failed to get manifest", reason "Failed to load manifest", solution "Check whether the
#   manifestUrl can be accessed normally":
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/troubleshooting/runtime.html
# - React lazy: "lazy lets you defer loading component's code until it is rendered for the first time":
#   https://react.dev/reference/react/lazy
# - Dynamic import: "dynamic imports are only evaluated when needed":
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/import
# Recordings from our example project (examples/host, remote-a, remote-b, built with "pnpm examples:build", served on
# ports 3000, 3001 and 3002 and loaded in jsdom, fresh page, nothing cached). They are not in recorded/outputs.json, which
# this lesson may not edit; the recording script is the same jsdom loader as examples/recorder/src/record.ts plus a request
# log on each server. jsdom is not a browser: the order of requests to different servers is not claimed, only the order
# on one server and what depends on what.
# - samples/request-log.txt: the requests each server saw, in order. The same order came out in three runs.
# - samples/deploy-remote-only.ts: remote_a's heading changed to "Remote a v2", only remote_a rebuilt, host untouched.
# - samples/grep-host-dist.ts: grep -rl "Remote a" on each dist folder.
# - samples/remote-b-down.ts: server on :3002 stopped, page empty after 8 s, RUNTIME-003 for the manifest of remote_b.
# - samples/host-index-html.ts and first-remote-request.ts: copied from host/dist/index.html and the "remotes" of
#   host/dist/mf-manifest.json.
# Labelled as our reading, not a documented guarantee: why the page stayed empty (we only record that it did), and
# that React's files came from :3002 in the recording (lesson 4 explains it).
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" concepts=["runtime-remote-loading", "host-and-remote"] %}
Friday, 16:55. The Remote a team deploys a fix. The Host team left at 16:30, so nobody rebuilds the host. The Bug has bet on which Remote a a fresh browser shows at 17:00.
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
Does the host contain Remote a's code, or only know where to find it?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/deploy-remote-only.ts" language="ts" concepts=["runtime-remote-loading", "host-and-remote"] %}
{% option output="Remote a" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
That is the old heading. It would stay only if the host had taken Remote a's code into its own build, and it did not.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Remote a v2" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The host fetched remote_a's manifest, which named the new file. No host rebuild was needed.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Remote b" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The host still renders both remotes. Nothing in a remote-only deploy removes remote_a from the page.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the host team can sleep through a Remote a deploy?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/config-excerpt.ts" concepts=["host-and-remote"] %}
The Module Federation glossary calls the app that exposes modules the **producer (remote)**, and the app that consumes them the **consumer (host)**. A remote lists modules under `exposes` ([docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/exposes.html)). A host lists remotes as `name@entry` under `remotes` ([docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/remotes.html)). The [glossary](https://module-federation.io/guide/start/glossary.html) has both terms.

{% annotation line=4 %}
The remote publishes this module.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=9 %}
Before the @: the remote's own name.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f1" estSeconds=60 template="// remote_a: publish a module\n___key___: { './Widget': './src/Widget.tsx' },\n\n// host: where does remote_a live?\nremotes: { remote_a: 'remote_a@http://localhost:3001/___file___' }," language="ts" concepts=["host-and-remote", "remote-entry-manifest"] %}
{% blank id="key" accepted=["exposes"] misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
exposes is what makes an app a remote. The docs say that setting exposes indicates a producer.
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="file" accepted=["mf-manifest.json"] misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The entry is the remote's manifest. The remotes docs allow mf-manifest.json or remoteEntry.js, and our remotes publish a manifest on every build.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/grep-host-dist.ts" language="ts" concepts=["runtime-remote-loading"] %}
{% option output="host/dist: 1 files contain \"Remote a\"" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
If the host had bundled the remote, its heading would be in a host file. The search found none.
{% /option %}
{% option output="host/dist: 0 files contain \"Remote a\"" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The host's build output has no code of Remote a. It holds the manifest address and nothing more.
{% /option %}
{% option output="host/dist: 2 files contain \"Remote a\"" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
Two files do contain the text, but both are in remote-a/dist. The host folder has none.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% predict id="p3" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/host-index-html.ts" language="ts" concepts=["request-order"] %}
{% option output="/mf-manifest.json" misconception="requests-in-parallel" %}
This HTML names no remote and no port 3001. Nothing in it can make the browser ask for a remote yet.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/static/js/index.da945bb8f4.js" correct=true %}
The HTML holds one script tag, the host's own. The browser has to run the host before it can learn about any remote.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/static/js/remote_a.71bd68a471.js" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The host never lists remote_a's file name. That name exists only in remote_a's own manifest.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the host page doesn't start the remote requests?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% predict id="p4" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/first-remote-request.ts" language="ts" concepts=["request-order", "remote-entry-manifest"] %}
{% option output="/static/js/remote_a.71bd68a471.js" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The host does not know that file name. Its config only holds the manifest address, so the runtime asks for the manifest.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/mf-manifest.json" correct=true %}
The host's only address for remote_a is the manifest. The remote entry's name comes out of it.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/static/js/async/__federation_expose_Widget.c2b648506a.js" misconception="requests-in-parallel" %}
The exposed module is not requested first and not alongside the manifest. Its file name is in the manifest too.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/request-log.txt" concepts=["request-order", "remote-entry-manifest"] %}
Per server, the order was the same in every run: manifest, remote entry, exposed module. The [manifest](https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html) is what a remote publishes on every build ([docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/manifest.html)), and the host's runtime reads it first. Our host files hold only the manifest address. In this recording, React came from :3002. Lesson 4 asks why.

{% annotation line=2 %}
The host's script holds both manifest addresses.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=4 %}
First request to a remote: its manifest.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=6 %}
The Widget code arrives last.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f2" estSeconds=45 template="const RemoteA = lazy(() => import('___spec___'));" language="ts" concepts=["runtime-remote-loading", "host-and-remote"] %}
{% blank id="spec" accepted=["remote_a/Widget"] misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The path is the remote's alias, a slash, and the exposed name without ./. It is a dynamic import, so the code is fetched when React first renders it.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p5" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/remote-b-down.ts" language="ts" concepts=["runtime-remote-loading"] %}
{% option output="Host, Remote a, Remote b" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The host does not have Remote b's code, so a stopped server matters. The build never contacted it, the browser did.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Host, Remote a, error box for remote_b" misconception="independent-deploy-is-risk-free" %}
That is what the example's error boundary is for, but in this recording it never got to show. Nothing rendered.
{% /option %}
{% option output="(empty page)" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. With only remote_b down, the host showed nothing, not even Remote a. The console had error RUNTIME-003.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
But Remote a was fine. Why does it vanish too?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% pitfall id="pf1" estSeconds=40 concepts=["runtime-remote-loading"] %}
CI was green at 17:02. At 17:05 the server behind Remote b restarted, and the host page went blank for everyone. We built our example host with no remote server running, so a green host build says nothing about remotes. The [troubleshooting page](https://module-federation.io/guide/troubleshooting/runtime.html) lists RUNTIME-003, "Failed to get manifest", and says to check the manifest URL.
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Green build, empty page. My favourite pair.
{% /bubble %}
{% gotcha %}
A host build never opens the remote. Only a browser, at runtime, finds out it is down.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /pitfall %}

{% fillBlanks id="f3" estSeconds=45 template="// A third remote, not part of our example. Its own config says name: 'remote_c'.\nremotes: {\n  remote_a: 'remote_a@http://localhost:3001/mf-manifest.json',\n  remote_c: '___name___@http://localhost:3003/mf-manifest.json',\n}," language="ts" concepts=["remote-entry-manifest", "host-and-remote"] %}
{% blank id="name" accepted=["remote_c"] misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
Before the @ goes the name the producer set in its own config. After it goes the manifest address.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% recap id="rc1" estSeconds=30 concepts=["host-and-remote", "runtime-remote-loading", "remote-entry-manifest", "request-order"] %}
{% point %}
A host holds only a remote's ==manifest address==. The remote's code stays on its own deployment.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Remotes are fetched at runtime, so a remote-only deploy reaches a fresh page without a host rebuild.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
The host's script runs first. Per remote the order is manifest, entry, then exposed module.
{% /point %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
You never rebuilt the host. I never left.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /recap %}

{% predict id="p6" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/widget-request.ts" language="ts" concepts=["request-order", "remote-entry-manifest"] %}
{% option output="/mf-manifest.json" misconception="host-bundles-remote" %}
The manifest describes the Widget, it does not contain it. It lists the files to fetch.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/static/js/remote_a.71bd68a471.js" misconception="requests-in-parallel" %}
The remote entry is requested before the Widget, but its text has no Remote a heading. It only loads the module.
{% /option %}
{% option output="/static/js/async/__federation_expose_Widget.c2b648506a.js" correct=true %}
The exposed module is its own chunk. It is the last request to :3001, and the only one whose file holds the heading.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% cliffhanger id="c1" estSeconds=20 nextLessonId="shared-deps-01" question="In our recording, React and React DOM came from remote_b's server, not from the host or remote_a. Who decides whose copy of React the page uses?" %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Next lesson I bring a second React. Hooks hate me.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /cliffhanger %}
